        "why": "The examiner looks for four things in the right places: outer and inner membrane on the envelope, grana as stacks inside, and stroma as the ground substance around them.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Drawing the grana outside the envelope. They lie inside the stroma.",
          "Drawing a single boundary line instead of a double membrane."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Draw the envelope, then the stroma, then the stacks of flat discs inside it.",
        "margin_note": "A 3-minute sketch. These labels are what carries the mark."
      },
      {
        "id": "s3_grana",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Thylakoids and grana",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Grana",
        "lines": [
          "Inside the stroma lie flattened membranous",
          "sacs called thylakoids. Thylakoids stacked",
          "one above another form a granum.",
          "Flat tubes called stroma lamellae join the",
          "thylakoids of different grana.",
          "The photosynthetic pigments, including the",
          "chlorophylls, lie in the thylakoid membrane."
        ],
        "why": "Stacking the thylakoids packs a very large membrane area into a small organelle, and that membrane is where the light reaction takes place.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Writing that grana are made of stroma lamellae. Lamellae connect grana; they are not the stacks.",
          "Placing the pigments in the stroma instead of the thylakoid membrane."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Thylakoid is one disc, granum is the stack, lamella is the flat tube that joins two stacks.",
        "margin_note": "Thylakoid, granum and lamella must all be named."
      },
      {
        "id": "s4_stroma",
        "kind": "boxed_final",
        "label": "Stroma contents",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Stroma",
        "lines": [
          {
            "text": "Chloroplast = envelope + grana + stroma",
            "style": "boxed"
          },
          "The stroma holds the enzymes of the dark",
          "reaction, a circular DNA molecule and 70S",
          "ribosomes.",
          "Starch granules and lipid droplets are stored",
          "in it.",
          "Because it has its own DNA and ribosomes the",
          "chloroplast is a semi-autonomous organelle."
        ],
        "why": "The DNA and the 70S ribosomes are the marked point: they are prokaryotic features, and they are why the chloroplast can make some of its own proteins and divide on its own.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Writing 80S ribosomes. The chloroplast has 70S, like a prokaryote.",
          "Leaving out the circular DNA, which is what makes it semi-autonomous."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Circular DNA plus 70S ribosomes equals semi-autonomous. The mitochondrion has exactly the same pair.",
        "margin_note": "The DNA and 70S ribosome line is the one most often missed."
      }
    ]
  },
  "insider_note": "The question never uses the word chloroplast, so open by naming it — the diagram and the grana description then carry most of the marks."
